<title>FRANCE: Group lodges challenge to French private pensions.</title>
<headline>Group lodges challenge to French private pensions.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-02-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>A group of opposition politicians has lodged a formal challenge with the French Constitutional Council to a law introducing private pension funds in France.</p>
<p>Roland Dumas, president of the Council, the country's top constitutional authority, reported the filing of the challenge to Philippe Seguin, leader of the National Assembly, or lower house of parliament, on Wednesday.</p>
<p>The opposition group comprises 78 Socialist, Communist and independent senators.</p>
<p>The legal challenge was submitted in a 25-page document which says several articles of the pensions law adopted last Thursday are not constitutional and that, among other things, the law would exacerbate inequality between high- and low-paid wage earners.</p>
</text>
